        free(buf);
        return NULL;
    }
    break;
}
```

When we hit EOF with no error, we `break` out of the loop. Then we do:
```c
buf[len] = '\0';
*len_out = len;
return buf;
```

So when we reach EOF mid-line (no trailing newline), we return the partial line. When we reach EOF after reading a complete line, we also return it. This should work...

But wait! There's a subtle bug. When `fgetc` returns EOF at the very beginning (empty input or file), we break with len=0, then we do `buf[0] = '\0'` and return the buffer. But this is wrong - we should return NULL to signal end of file when there's no data!

